course: dictionary
lesson: 6678
title: Oblige: force, help or thank
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Oblige is a verb with three meanings, so the sentence around it tells you which one is meant. Read the words before and after it to find the clue. «Pupils are obliged to wear uniform.» Here the school rule is the clue: obliged means made to do something by a rule or duty.
explain: «Could you oblige me with a pen?» Here oblige means to help someone by doing what they ask. The question tells you that someone is asking, so you can choose to say yes.
explain: «I'm much obliged» is an old-fashioned phrase that means thank you very much. Check it by swapping in thank you: "I'm much thank you" does not fit, so read the whole phrase as a set of words that means thanks.
fact: To oblige someone can mean to force them to do something by law, a rule or a duty.
fact: To oblige someone can also mean to help them by doing what they ask.
fact: «I'm much obliged» is formal and old-fashioned, and it means thank you very much.
fact: Learn these phrases: be obliged to, feel obliged to, and happy to oblige.
fact: The verb changes its form: oblige, obliges, obliged, obliging.
exercise: What does oblige mean in «Pupils are obliged to wear uniform.»? | Left behind at school | Happy to try something new | Given a present | *Made to do it by a rule
exercise: What does oblige mean in «Could you oblige me with a pen?» | *Help someone by doing what they ask | Make someone pay for a pen | Lose something important | Write a long letter
exercise: Find and copy the phrase that means thank you very much. | *I'm much obliged | feel obliged to | be obliged to | happy to oblige
test: Mum says Ben is ___ to tidy his room before he plays. | *obliged | oblige | obliging | obliges
test: Our neighbour is very ___ and always helps carry the shopping. | obliged | *obliging | obliges | oblige
exam: Read «Would you oblige me by closing the door?» What does oblige mean here? | To lose something | To force someone by law | To say thank you very much | *To help someone by doing what they ask
